On 10 November 2026 OpenAI announced Businesses in ChatGPT, a beta that lets a company claim its presence inside ChatGPT, keep its details up to date and see how often it shows up. If you run a small shop or an online service, the practical questions are who can join, what the setup involves, and what you do not get. This post answers them from OpenAI's announcement and its help article, and marks what those pages leave out.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited OpenAI pages for figures, dates and wording. This is not a full human review. OpenAI makes ChatGPT, which competes with Claude, so read any comparison with that in mind. Stackbrook did not test Businesses in ChatGPT and has no affiliate relationship with any company mentioned at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated.*

## What OpenAI says it is

OpenAI describes the program as a way for businesses to reach the 1.2 billion people it says use ChatGPT each week. The announcement describes four things. Business Hub is where you claim and verify your business, update key details and add product feeds with pricing and availability. Insights show aggregated figures on mentions, website clicks, profile views and "discovery topics", meaning recurring themes in answers that cite your website. Business Agents let a claimed business build, test and launch an agent that answers customer questions. Sponsored agents let a business sponsor its agent and use it as the destination for ChatGPT Ads, with sponsored placements labelled and kept separate from ChatGPT's answers.

The announcement calls the whole program a beta. The help article, by contrast, does not label any feature as beta, so the status of individual features is not spelled out.

## Who can claim a profile

According to the announcement, any eligible business in a place where ChatGPT is available can claim its profile through Business Hub. Enterprise customers and advertisers can work with their OpenAI representative instead, and Shopify merchants can connect a store from Business Hub or the Shopify admin. The help article adds that you must own the business or be authorised to represent it, and that you do not need an ads account or a ChatGPT Enterprise agreement. It also says availability depends on the business's location and type, and that local stores and restaurants can claim only an existing US listing.

Business Agents are narrower. Both pages say they are for eligible ecommerce and online service businesses in the US, and the help article adds that the business needs a website and must meet the industry requirements in OpenAI's Business Agents Policy.

## The setup path

The help article walks through the claim in steps, which the chart below condenses. You sign in to ChatGPT, open Business Hub, find your business listing and confirm which ChatGPT account you will use. You then pick a verification method: a code by text or call to the business phone, a code sent to the business email, a DNS TXT record on your website domain, or a Shopify store connection. OpenAI reviews the claim and emails a decision or a request for more information.

![Flow diagram of the Businesses in ChatGPT claim path: open Business Hub, verify, ID check with Persona, update the profile, then optional product feed and agent](/images/site1/businesses-in-chatgpt-small-business-explained-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the cited pages.*

A separate identity check through a company called Persona is required to publish changes, connect a product feed, view insights or create an agent. It may ask for a government-issued ID, business registration details and proof that you are authorised to act for the business. Approval is not automatic. After that you fill in the profile: website, description, category, logo, delivery policy, return policy and optional extras such as a cover photo. A product feed, either a CSV that follows OpenAI's specification or a hosted URL, and a business agent are both optional.

## What you get once claimed

The help article says a profile lets you manage the information ChatGPT can use about your business. Insights cover the past 28 days. A verified badge, shown as a blue check, appears only after Persona approval, and claiming alone does not add it.

Mentions are counted narrowly. For an online business, ChatGPT must reference your website, and the brand name alone does not count. For a local business, a mention counts when ChatGPT shows a result for that location. The article warns that blank metrics may mean a reporting threshold has not been reached, data is still being collected or reporting is unavailable, so a blank is not necessarily zero activity.

## The limits worth knowing before you start

The help article is blunt about several limits. An agent cannot complete purchases, make bookings or check a customer's order, though it can link customers to your website. It does not use data from a ChatGPT plugin you may have. Each distinct brand is managed separately, and central management across a parent company's brands and locations is not currently available. Claiming a main brand does not claim its locations.

On visibility, OpenAI says claiming gives no guarantee of more visibility and no control over ChatGPT's own recommendations, and that advertising does not influence those answers. Profile updates are reviewed and can take time to appear. Only an unpublished draft profile can be deleted: OpenAI Support cannot delete, unpublish or hide a published profile as part of a setup or ownership request. That is worth weighing before you publish anything.

## Privacy and what is not stated

OpenAI says it does not share users' conversations, or chats with Business Agents, with businesses, and that insights are aggregated and appear only once certain thresholds are met. The pages do not give those thresholds. They also give no price for claiming, insights, agents or sponsorship, and beyond Enterprise customers and advertisers they do not say which ChatGPT plans, if any, are involved. OpenAI says it plans to expand to more markets and business categories over time, with no dates given.

## What to do with this

If you are a US ecommerce or online service business, the claim flow involves verification and an ID check, and a published profile cannot be removed through support, so read the Business Agents Policy and the help article first. Outside the US, or for a local shop, you may be limited to claiming a profile. Because this is a fast-changing beta, check OpenAI's live pages for the current rules. This is general information, not legal or financial advice.
